WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:07.240
Structured and binary input should be parsed as a series of verified facts, not as a blob that can be cast into a structure and trusted.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:15.860
Records, headers, magic values, version fields, lengths, counts, offsets, and flags all need validation before they influence memory access.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:22.400
A magic value may confirm that the file is probably the expected format, but it does not prove that the rest of the file is safe.

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:30.540
A version field may select a parser, but it should also constrain which fields are legal for that version. Embedded size fields are a common source of vulnerabilities.

00:00:30.760 --> 00:00:39.240
A file or packet can claim to contain a thousand records when the available data only holds three. A length can point beyond the end of a buffer.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:44.660
An offset can point backward, overlap another region, or wrap when combined with a length.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:57.200
Before allocating, copying, or advancing a pointer, check that each referenced region is inside the actual input and inside your configured limits. Do not multiply counts by record sizes without checking overflow first.

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:05.580
Binary parsing also needs explicit decisions about byte order, alignment, optional fields, duplicate fields, and invalid combinations.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:11.160
Checksums can help detect accidental corruption, but they do not make untrusted input trustworthy.

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:19.140
Defensive parsers separate reading from validation, maintain a clear parsing state, and reject inconsistent structures before using their contents.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:30.400
The practical rule is simple: no memory access should depend on a length, count, offset, or type value until that value has been checked against both the format rules and the bytes actually present.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:36.060
The example treats its input as one complete packet, so undeclared trailing bytes are rejected.

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:47.800
Its returned payload pointer is a borrow into the caller-owned input and remains valid only while that input stays alive and unmodified. A streaming parser would instead return the exact number of bytes consumed.
